WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.959 --> 00:00:05.627
Pat Mitchell: Welcome, Jane.

00:00:05.669 --> 00:00:07.128
The last time you joined us --

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:08.338
Jane Fonda: Hi, Pat.

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:10.590
PM: It's so good to see you, as always.

00:00:10.632 --> 00:00:13.051
The last time you joined us
on TEDWomen, Jane,

00:00:13.093 --> 00:00:14.970
I was thinking about that this morning,

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:18.139
you were in the nation's
capital, Washington, DC,

00:00:18.181 --> 00:00:22.519
and you were leading the weekly protests,
Fire [Drill] Fridays --

00:00:22.561 --> 00:00:24.563
Fire Drill Fridays,

00:00:24.604 --> 00:00:27.649
in partnership with
the Greenpeace International.

00:00:28.024 --> 00:00:30.360
You got arrested, it made headlines.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:35.031
and certainly, all of the civil
disobedience actions

00:00:35.073 --> 00:00:37.367
brought attention to the crisis.

00:00:37.409 --> 00:00:42.998
And I understand that you are restarting
Fire Drill Fridays in December.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.917
What are your goals and expectations?

00:00:46.835 --> 00:00:52.591
JF: Well, our goal with Fire Drill Fridays
was and continues to be

00:00:52.591 --> 00:00:56.636
to move people from concern
about the climate crisis

00:00:56.636 --> 00:00:59.055
to action about the climate crisis.

00:00:59.097 --> 00:01:00.515
And we've been successful.

00:01:00.557 --> 00:01:04.519
We're coming back
on December 2 to Washington, DC,

00:01:04.561 --> 00:01:06.521
for our first live, in-person rally,

00:01:06.563 --> 00:01:10.191
which will address the outcome
of the midterm elections.

00:01:10.233 --> 00:01:16.239
And we'll talk about what climate activism
needs to look like in 2023.

00:01:16.239 --> 00:01:19.034
But, you know, Pat,
we never stopped the weekly rallies.

00:01:19.075 --> 00:01:22.454
When COVID hit, we went online
with Fire Drill Fridays,

00:01:22.495 --> 00:01:26.207
and last week we had our
11 millionth viewer.

00:01:26.207 --> 00:01:27.375
It's working.

00:01:27.417 --> 00:01:32.505
People who had never taken action before
have been trained by Greenpeace US

00:01:32.505 --> 00:01:36.009
to write and call and visit
their representatives.

00:01:36.468 --> 00:01:38.762
They follow up on our calls to action,

00:01:38.762 --> 00:01:43.016
they participate in getting out the vote
and volunteering with candidates.

00:01:43.016 --> 00:01:45.268
For those in other countries,
you should know,

00:01:45.268 --> 00:01:48.521
we have a very critical
midterm elections coming up

00:01:48.521 --> 00:01:50.065
in a couple of weeks.

00:01:51.232 --> 00:01:53.777
Anyway, so our people
are becoming activists,

00:01:53.818 --> 00:01:57.947
and Fire Drill Fridays, by the way,
has become part of Greenpeace.

00:01:57.947 --> 00:01:59.699
And we're very honored by that.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:03.662
PM: Well, there is no question
that it had impact.

00:02:03.662 --> 00:02:07.707
And it's really encouraging to know
that you're bringing back the actions

00:02:07.749 --> 00:02:09.459
and convening us again.

00:02:09.459 --> 00:02:12.420
And I'll certainly try and be there, Jane,

00:02:12.462 --> 00:02:15.882
to participate in Fire Drill Fridays.

00:02:15.924 --> 00:02:18.093
And you mentioned the elections,

00:02:18.093 --> 00:02:22.430
very important elections happening
in the United States in November.

00:02:22.472 --> 00:02:25.350
And you have taken some additional action,

00:02:25.392 --> 00:02:31.356
another priority, by creating what we call
a political action committee, a PAC.

00:02:31.648 --> 00:02:34.067
Explain your expectations

00:02:34.109 --> 00:02:37.612
and why you see this
as a necessary action.

00:02:38.196 --> 00:02:41.700
JF: Well, after decades of marching
and protesting and petitioning

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:43.576
and civil disobedience,

00:02:43.618 --> 00:02:46.955
we still are not getting
the kind of climate legislation

00:02:46.996 --> 00:02:48.915
that is absolutely critical.

00:02:52.043 --> 00:02:54.838
What we need to do is reduce
fossil fuel emissions.

00:02:54.838 --> 00:03:00.343
And, you know, recently in the US,
we got the Inflation Reduction Act,

00:03:00.385 --> 00:03:04.180
which puts a lot of money in renewables
like electric vehicles

00:03:04.222 --> 00:03:05.807
and the grid

00:03:05.849 --> 00:03:07.892
and solar panels and so forth.

00:03:07.934 --> 00:03:13.022
But too much legislation that would
have been important has failed

00:03:13.064 --> 00:03:17.986
because both Republicans and Democrats
take money from the fossil fuel industry.

00:03:17.986 --> 00:03:22.282
And I know this is a huge problem
in all oil and gas producing countries.

00:03:23.491 --> 00:03:28.121
And so earlier this year, we launched
the Jane Fonda Climate PAC

00:03:28.163 --> 00:03:32.751
with the unique goal of electing
climate champions to office,

00:03:32.751 --> 00:03:38.798
not just top governmental office,
but state and local offices

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:41.009
which turn out to be really,
really important.

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:44.596
You know, we have to break
the stranglehold

00:03:44.637 --> 00:03:47.348
that the fossil fuel industry
has on our government,

00:03:47.348 --> 00:03:49.267
not just here in North America,

00:03:49.267 --> 00:03:50.435
but globally.

00:03:50.477 --> 00:03:54.898
And that's why we're moving climate
activism into the electoral arena,

00:03:54.939 --> 00:03:59.194
which is kind of unique
because we focus down ballot

00:03:59.235 --> 00:04:03.656
and because we're trying to change
the narrative about fossil fuels.

00:04:03.698 --> 00:04:07.786
You know, everybody talks
about wind turbines and solar panels

00:04:07.786 --> 00:04:09.954
and electric vehicles,

00:04:09.954 --> 00:04:11.706
but that's not enough.

00:04:12.457 --> 00:04:17.045
And my PAC is only endorsing candidates
who have signed a pledge

00:04:17.086 --> 00:04:19.714
to not take money
from fossil fuel industry

00:04:19.756 --> 00:04:22.050
and who've publicly stood up
and showed courage

00:04:22.091 --> 00:04:24.302
in the face of the industry

00:04:24.302 --> 00:04:27.889
and not afraid of holding
them accountable.

00:04:30.016 --> 00:04:36.147
PM: And that was the criteria
on which you selected the candidates.

00:04:36.189 --> 00:04:40.485
I've also noticed some of them,
maybe most of them are women.

00:04:40.527 --> 00:04:44.239
Seeing women taking
those front line positions.

00:04:44.531 --> 00:04:45.865
JF: Women of color.

00:04:47.617 --> 00:04:51.371
Very often they're the first --
the first woman, the first Latina,

00:04:51.412 --> 00:04:53.456
the first person under 30,

00:04:53.498 --> 00:04:55.583
they’re really breaking
a lot of boundaries,

00:04:55.583 --> 00:04:57.210
and they're so brave.

00:04:57.627 --> 00:04:58.837
I was just in Texas,

00:04:58.878 --> 00:05:02.090
and it's filled me with hope
that will last me for years.

00:05:03.508 --> 00:05:07.053
PM: Well, I want to come back
to how you keep that hope alive, Jane,

00:05:07.095 --> 00:05:11.474
because it's easy to look at the fact

00:05:11.516 --> 00:05:14.435
that climate is not a partisan issue.

00:05:14.477 --> 00:05:16.020
And yet, as you said,

00:05:16.062 --> 00:05:20.066
leaders on both sides of the aisle
are still taking the money

00:05:20.066 --> 00:05:23.820
that's leading to the carbon
emissions challenge.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:27.615
Why really, do you have a better
understanding now

00:05:27.615 --> 00:05:31.828
and can you share it, of why
we have such slow progress

00:05:31.828 --> 00:05:34.998
on addressing this urgent global crisis?

00:05:35.832 --> 00:05:38.376
JF: Well, I think a lot of it
has to do with the fact

00:05:38.376 --> 00:05:42.839
that the fossil fuel industry
is embedded in our government.

00:05:42.881 --> 00:05:45.550
People on both sides
of the aisles are taking money,

00:05:45.592 --> 00:05:48.970
and so they don't speak out
against the fossil fuel industry.

00:05:49.012 --> 00:05:51.681
They only talk
about the green alternatives,

00:05:51.723 --> 00:05:53.141
and that's important.

00:05:53.141 --> 00:05:58.521
But not talking about fossil fuels
is like trying to bail out a sinking boat

00:05:58.563 --> 00:06:01.733
without plugging the hole, you know.

00:06:02.984 --> 00:06:07.780
But there's been progress because more
and more people do understand that,

00:06:07.822 --> 00:06:10.366
that it is fossil fuels
that are driving --

00:06:10.366 --> 00:06:13.620
burning of fossil fuels
that's driving the climate crisis.

00:06:13.661 --> 00:06:16.956
And this is really, really important.

00:06:16.998 --> 00:06:18.333
The science is very clear.

00:06:18.333 --> 00:06:23.296
We have seven years to cut
our fossil fuel emissions in half

00:06:23.296 --> 00:06:29.218
in order to keep our global warming
no higher than 1.5 degrees Celsius,

00:06:29.218 --> 00:06:32.263
higher than it was before
the Industrial Revolution,

00:06:32.305 --> 00:06:34.807
which is when we started
to burn fossil fuels.

00:06:36.351 --> 00:06:39.145
So, I mean, I think there has
been progress locally,

00:06:39.145 --> 00:06:42.523
especially in states and cities.

00:06:42.565 --> 00:06:45.318
They've made progress to reduce
their carbon footprint.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:47.528
It's just that more needs
to be done urgently.

00:06:47.528 --> 00:06:49.864
And the reason that I'm talking about this

00:06:49.906 --> 00:06:53.743
on an international forum
like you've provided,

00:06:53.785 --> 00:06:56.412
is because I think
that a lot of countries,

00:06:56.412 --> 00:06:58.539
I'm thinking of Nigeria, for example,

00:06:58.539 --> 00:07:01.376
have the same problems
that the United States does,

00:07:01.376 --> 00:07:02.752
they produce oil,

00:07:02.794 --> 00:07:07.799
and that we need to be thinking more
about taking the climate activism

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:10.051
into the electoral arena, you know,

00:07:10.051 --> 00:07:12.887
recruit candidates and then train them

00:07:12.929 --> 00:07:15.765
and teach them about climate science,

00:07:15.807 --> 00:07:18.726
and then get them to run for office
and make sure they win.

00:07:18.768 --> 00:07:21.604
This is really what the strategy is now.

00:07:22.897 --> 00:07:24.357
PM: And you're so right, Jane,

00:07:24.399 --> 00:07:29.404
that is a strategy that could be
exported to every country,

00:07:29.404 --> 00:07:33.366
because in every country,
or in most every country,

00:07:33.408 --> 00:07:34.867
there are elections.

00:07:34.909 --> 00:07:38.746
And people can make a difference
by the candidates they support

00:07:38.788 --> 00:07:40.873
and encourage to run.

00:07:41.416 --> 00:07:42.583
JF: Yeah.

00:07:43.167 --> 00:07:47.463
PM: I do think often of your activism

00:07:47.505 --> 00:07:49.632
and how you've sustained it,

00:07:49.674 --> 00:07:53.302
especially when it comes to this issue,
which sometimes feels intractable,

00:07:53.344 --> 00:07:54.971
although we know it isn't.

00:07:55.013 --> 00:07:57.849
But when you hear scientists
say seven years,

00:07:57.849 --> 00:08:00.685
we're this far away, we haven't done this,

00:08:00.685 --> 00:08:02.729
we must do that,

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:04.313
it's easy to lose hope.

00:08:04.355 --> 00:08:07.942
It's easy to let fear that we can't do it

00:08:07.984 --> 00:08:11.571
replace the hope
necessary to ignite action.

00:08:11.571 --> 00:08:13.531
So what gives you hope?

00:08:14.699 --> 00:08:19.871
JF: Well, as I said, we have elections
here in the US in less than two weeks.

00:08:19.912 --> 00:08:23.750
And I've been traveling the country
supporting amazing candidates

00:08:23.791 --> 00:08:26.044
that my climate PAC is endorsing.

00:08:26.085 --> 00:08:28.171
You know, we don't have enough money yet,

00:08:28.171 --> 00:08:29.422
we're a new PAC,

00:08:29.464 --> 00:08:34.969
to play in the Senate arena
or the governorship arena,

00:08:35.011 --> 00:08:38.473
which, you know, they cost
a lot of money, those races.

00:08:38.473 --> 00:08:41.559
But the down ballot races, you know,

00:08:41.601 --> 00:08:45.438
the ones in the state legislatures
and county supervisors

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:49.776
and attorneys general,
things like that, are so important.

00:08:49.817 --> 00:08:54.864
And every dollar that goes to those
down ballot races means a lot.

00:08:54.864 --> 00:08:59.786
For for instance, in Texas,
the Railroad Commission, oddly,

00:08:59.786 --> 00:09:04.082
is in charge of regulating and overseeing
the oil and gas industry.

00:09:04.123 --> 00:09:09.545
You know, historically it was
supposed to regulate the railroad,

00:09:09.545 --> 00:09:13.966
but then when Texas became, you know,
the massive oil and gas producer,

00:09:14.008 --> 00:09:18.054
they switched the responsibilities
of the Railroad Commission to oil and gas,

00:09:18.096 --> 00:09:19.764
but they didn't change the name.

00:09:19.806 --> 00:09:23.184
So a lot of people don't even know
what the Railroad Commission does.

00:09:23.184 --> 00:09:26.437
And historically, all the commissioners,
there's three of them,

00:09:26.479 --> 00:09:30.650
they've worked for the very industry
that they're supposed to regulate, right?

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:32.401
Which means they do nothing.

00:09:33.319 --> 00:09:36.656
I don't know if people
that are watching know about this

00:09:36.697 --> 00:09:38.157
or read about it in the paper,

00:09:38.157 --> 00:09:40.660
but two years ago,
when there was an extreme freeze

00:09:40.701 --> 00:09:44.413
and the electric generators
failed to produce enough power

00:09:44.455 --> 00:09:46.833
to keep the lights on
and the heat running,

00:09:46.874 --> 00:09:50.044
I talked to neighbors
who were found frozen to death

00:09:50.086 --> 00:09:51.504
in their own living rooms,

00:09:51.546 --> 00:09:53.631
and none of it needed to happen.

00:09:53.673 --> 00:09:56.050
Extreme weather had shut down
the grid before,

00:09:56.050 --> 00:09:59.554
the Railroad Commission had been warned
to weatherize to the grid

00:09:59.595 --> 00:10:00.763
and nothing was done.

00:10:00.805 --> 00:10:02.974
And still the grid
hasn’t been weatherized.

00:10:02.974 --> 00:10:06.352
And so it remains very vulnerable
to another disaster.

00:10:06.394 --> 00:10:10.898
So my PAC is endorsing
just as one example,

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:14.443
a young progressive Democrat,
happens to be very handsome,

00:10:14.485 --> 00:10:15.736
named Luke Warford,

00:10:15.778 --> 00:10:17.363
he's 33 years old,

00:10:17.363 --> 00:10:21.200
he’s not afraid to hold the oil and gas
industry accountable --

00:10:21.242 --> 00:10:25.371
not to build a career
but to really make a difference.

00:10:25.413 --> 00:10:27.331
And if he's elected,

00:10:27.373 --> 00:10:30.751
it’s going to change
how the Commission does its job.

00:10:30.751 --> 00:10:34.213
And believe me, Texans
and the planet will be safer.

00:10:34.714 --> 00:10:37.216
Not only has he pledged
to weatherize the grid,

00:10:37.216 --> 00:10:41.137
he's going to enforce
regulations against flaring,

00:10:41.179 --> 00:10:43.556
which is a practice of burning excess gas

00:10:43.598 --> 00:10:46.309
that releases methane into the atmosphere.

00:10:46.309 --> 00:10:47.935
And as you all probably know,

00:10:47.977 --> 00:10:50.646
methane is the most damaging
greenhouse gas,

00:10:50.646 --> 00:10:53.941
and Texas releases more of it
than any other state.

00:10:53.941 --> 00:10:58.404
So this can all change if this young guy
is elected to office.

00:10:58.446 --> 00:11:01.073
And then in Harris County, Texas,

00:11:01.073 --> 00:11:04.994
Harris County is enormous,
and it is ground zero

00:11:04.994 --> 00:11:07.079
for the climate crisis in North America.

00:11:07.413 --> 00:11:10.917
And this woman immigrated
with her family from Colombia,

00:11:10.958 --> 00:11:16.464
she was elected as the top Harris County
executive, Judge Lina Hidalgo,

00:11:16.505 --> 00:11:19.133
the first female and the first Latina

00:11:19.175 --> 00:11:24.055
to ever hold that particular
incredibly important office.

00:11:24.430 --> 00:11:28.267
And she became a citizen
of the US in 2013,

00:11:28.309 --> 00:11:30.895
and she got elected in 2018.

00:11:30.937 --> 00:11:33.773
You know, nobody
took her seriously, you know.

00:11:33.773 --> 00:11:36.567
Her opponent didn't even
bother to campaign.

00:11:36.609 --> 00:11:40.529
But in the four years since
she was elected to office,

00:11:40.571 --> 00:11:45.076
she has blown everybody's mind
by how much she's accomplished.

00:11:45.117 --> 00:11:48.204
She's holding the fossil fuel
industry accountable,

00:11:48.204 --> 00:11:50.122
she's made it easier to vote,

00:11:50.122 --> 00:11:52.750
she stopped a highway
going through Houston.

00:11:52.750 --> 00:11:55.920
She's changed the way the county works.

00:11:55.920 --> 00:11:58.881
She controls a four-billion-dollar budget

00:11:58.881 --> 00:12:00.841
and has a robust climate plan

00:12:00.841 --> 00:12:06.639
to change how Harris County
deals with fossil fuels.

00:12:06.639 --> 00:12:10.977
You know, she believes
that the energy capital of the world

00:12:11.018 --> 00:12:15.439
can also become the renewable energy
capital of the world.

00:12:15.481 --> 00:12:17.275
And I've seen in the last month

00:12:17.316 --> 00:12:22.530
how a big difference my new climate PAC
is having on down ballot races

00:12:22.530 --> 00:12:24.448
like Luke's and Lina's.

00:12:24.448 --> 00:12:26.659
And this is what thrills me.

00:12:26.701 --> 00:12:28.244
This is what gives me hope.

00:12:28.286 --> 00:12:32.373
I believe the most important thing
that I've ever done is creating this PAC,

00:12:32.373 --> 00:12:35.126
and it's what I'm going
to continue to do till I die.

00:12:36.585 --> 00:12:40.548
PM: Jane, those kind of stories
are what we need to hear.

00:12:40.589 --> 00:12:45.928
I mean, how inspiring is that, that one
single individual has made that difference

00:12:45.970 --> 00:12:47.138
in their community?

00:12:47.179 --> 00:12:49.056
And I want to come back to that.

00:12:49.849 --> 00:12:52.935
JF: It’s as though
Vanessa Nakate ran for office,

00:12:52.935 --> 00:12:54.103
you know what I mean?

00:12:54.145 --> 00:12:55.646
What would that look like?

00:12:55.688 --> 00:12:57.148
And I'm putting that out there

00:12:57.148 --> 00:13:00.318
because maybe she could consider
doing that, you know.

00:13:00.359 --> 00:13:02.236
PM: I hope Vanessa is listening,

00:13:02.278 --> 00:13:05.489
and if she is, we'll make sure
that she gets this message.

00:13:06.866 --> 00:13:09.827
Jane, we have a question from Kat,

00:13:09.869 --> 00:13:14.415
who is joining us from the TEDxWomen
in Vail, Colorado.

00:13:14.749 --> 00:13:19.545
And you may have done this just now,
but I'm sure there are others, she asked,

00:13:19.587 --> 00:13:24.675
"Could you describe what are your most
joyful moments as a climate activist?"

00:13:27.595 --> 00:13:30.389
JF: Well, frankly, I got back
from Texas yesterday,

00:13:30.431 --> 00:13:33.225
and my body is still humming

00:13:33.267 --> 00:13:38.689
with the thrill of working
with these young people.

00:13:38.731 --> 00:13:41.442
You know, and it had happened
two weeks ago in New Mexico

00:13:41.442 --> 00:13:43.986
with a young woman
who's the public land commissioner,

00:13:43.986 --> 00:13:45.571
also a Latina woman.

00:13:46.113 --> 00:13:48.240
And in Michigan with the attorney general,

00:13:48.282 --> 00:13:49.867
Dana Nessel.

00:13:49.909 --> 00:13:51.786
They are so brave,

00:13:51.827 --> 00:13:56.290
and they so understand
that fossil fuels is what’s killing us.

00:13:56.290 --> 00:13:59.460
And they’re not afraid to stand up

00:13:59.502 --> 00:14:02.171
to the big corporations.

00:14:02.213 --> 00:14:03.547
And it just makes me --

00:14:03.589 --> 00:14:06.634
I can see the future when I'm with them.

00:14:06.634 --> 00:14:08.886
That's what gives me hope.

00:14:10.096 --> 00:14:11.555
PM: And joy.

00:14:12.431 --> 00:14:15.726
We hear from a lot of people,
though, you know,

00:14:15.768 --> 00:14:18.354
will say back to a story like that,

00:14:18.396 --> 00:14:20.648
well, that person's an elected official

00:14:20.689 --> 00:14:23.901
or they've already taken
a leadership position.

00:14:23.901 --> 00:14:26.695
And often you'll hear individuals say,

00:14:26.737 --> 00:14:29.990
"I just don't know what I can do

00:14:30.032 --> 00:14:32.326
as an individual in the community."

00:14:32.368 --> 00:14:34.203
How do you respond to that?

00:14:34.203 --> 00:14:37.248
JF: Right, well, that's a really,
really important question.

00:14:37.289 --> 00:14:41.710
The truth is, there is limited impact
that we can have alone,

00:14:41.752 --> 00:14:44.797
and there is infinite impact
we can have together.

00:14:45.256 --> 00:14:48.426
So the most important thing
that any of us can do besides voting

00:14:48.467 --> 00:14:49.802
is to join with others,

00:14:49.802 --> 00:14:54.181
to share our passion
so that we can have a bigger impact.

00:14:54.223 --> 00:14:57.768
You know, join an organization,
join a climate group,

00:14:57.810 --> 00:15:00.563
get your community or your church group

00:15:00.604 --> 00:15:02.231
active on climate.

00:15:02.273 --> 00:15:05.568
And you know, the great thing
about doing this work with others,

00:15:05.568 --> 00:15:08.154
in addition to having a bigger impact,

00:15:08.154 --> 00:15:10.823
it's more fun, it's more rewarding.

00:15:10.823 --> 00:15:12.575
We make new friends.

00:15:12.575 --> 00:15:17.246
But to answer many people's questions,

00:15:17.288 --> 00:15:19.582
when it comes to individual choices,

00:15:19.623 --> 00:15:21.709
a recent study has shown

00:15:21.750 --> 00:15:24.086
that there is one big impact

00:15:24.128 --> 00:15:27.548
of making climate-friendly
lifestyle choices.

00:15:27.548 --> 00:15:31.385
Things like, you know,
don't use single-use plastics anymore,

00:15:31.385 --> 00:15:34.555
you know, ride a bike
instead of driving a car, etc.

00:15:35.806 --> 00:15:39.435
It increases your credibility
as an advocate

00:15:39.435 --> 00:15:43.355
for the kind of bigger
and bolder change that's needed.

00:15:43.355 --> 00:15:45.524
So, you know, for example,

00:15:45.524 --> 00:15:48.152
Greta Thunberg sailed across the ocean.

00:15:48.569 --> 00:15:53.157
This had no impact on actual
carbon emissions and climate change,

00:15:53.199 --> 00:15:58.037
but it had an impact on people
taking her message more seriously.

00:15:58.454 --> 00:16:00.623
And that's a really interesting finding.

00:16:00.623 --> 00:16:04.460
So what follows then is that the way
to increase the impact

00:16:04.502 --> 00:16:06.712
of our individual choices

00:16:06.712 --> 00:16:09.089
is to be sure that they’re visible.

00:16:09.131 --> 00:16:12.259
So that if you replace your old
gas-powered water heater

00:16:12.259 --> 00:16:15.012
with an electric heat pump, for example,

00:16:15.012 --> 00:16:16.805
or you install solar panels

00:16:16.805 --> 00:16:20.142
or you use a reusable water bottle,

00:16:20.184 --> 00:16:21.810
make it visible.

00:16:21.852 --> 00:16:26.440
Tell your neighbors,
post pictures on social media.

00:16:26.482 --> 00:16:29.276
You don’t have to be self-righteous
or braggy about it,

00:16:29.276 --> 00:16:31.153
but in an inviting way.

00:16:31.195 --> 00:16:32.696
"Hey, folks, here's an idea."

00:16:32.738 --> 00:16:34.448
You know, make it visible.

00:16:35.157 --> 00:16:36.659
PM: And make it personal.

00:16:36.659 --> 00:16:39.828
As Mary Robinson also
always encourages us,

00:16:39.870 --> 00:16:45.251
do something personal everyday
that moves us forward to a better time.

00:16:45.668 --> 00:16:48.963
There's another question, Jane,
that comes from Dallas, Texas.

00:16:49.004 --> 00:16:52.216
So I think people in Texas
are paying attention

00:16:52.258 --> 00:16:54.260
to the stories you've just shared.

00:16:54.301 --> 00:16:55.427
She says,

00:16:55.469 --> 00:16:59.139
"It's so important to be able
to identify climate activists,

00:16:59.181 --> 00:17:02.184
the ones that will really
fight for legislation

00:17:02.226 --> 00:17:04.103
to combat the climate crisis.

00:17:04.144 --> 00:17:07.690
So how do we, meaning
we at large in a community,

00:17:07.731 --> 00:17:09.483
know and back candidates

00:17:09.525 --> 00:17:12.236
who are going to actually
fight for change?

00:17:12.278 --> 00:17:15.573
Is there certain criteria
that you're using

00:17:15.614 --> 00:17:17.741
and that would stand out for anyone

00:17:17.783 --> 00:17:20.744
who claims to fight
for green legislation?"

00:17:21.328 --> 00:17:22.496
JF: Yeah.

00:17:22.538 --> 00:17:26.542
Number one, do they take money
from the fossil fuel industry?

00:17:26.584 --> 00:17:27.751
That is a no-no,

00:17:27.793 --> 00:17:29.920
because that means that they're not free

00:17:29.962 --> 00:17:32.923
to really stand up
to the fossil fuel industry.

00:17:32.923 --> 00:17:35.342
And here in the United States,
in North America,

00:17:35.342 --> 00:17:38.345
we have what's called
the fossil fuel pledge,

00:17:38.345 --> 00:17:43.017
where many, many, many politicians
and candidates sign a pledge

00:17:43.058 --> 00:17:47.521
saying that they won't take money
from these big corporations.

00:17:48.063 --> 00:17:50.941
And then the other thing that we look for:

00:17:50.983 --> 00:17:55.946
Has the candidate shown courage
by standing up publicly in some way?

00:17:55.946 --> 00:17:58.532
Have they opposed an oil pipeline?

00:17:58.574 --> 00:18:02.494
Have they opposed something
that might pollute the waterways

00:18:02.536 --> 00:18:04.079
in your town or city?

00:18:05.956 --> 00:18:09.543
Doing something publicly shows
that you have real courage

00:18:09.543 --> 00:18:12.588
and you're willing to stand up and fight.

00:18:14.340 --> 00:18:16.592
PM: And looking
for the candidates who do that

00:18:16.634 --> 00:18:18.385
and then working for them,

00:18:18.427 --> 00:18:22.806
supporting them
and encouraging them to run.

00:18:22.806 --> 00:18:25.351
And that can, as you have reminded us,

00:18:25.351 --> 00:18:26.894
happen anywhere.

00:18:26.935 --> 00:18:28.145
We have another question.

00:18:28.187 --> 00:18:30.230
This one comes from New Jersey.

00:18:30.272 --> 00:18:32.650
“So many young people
have now taken a stand

00:18:32.650 --> 00:18:34.735
to support climate initiatives.

00:18:34.777 --> 00:18:39.657
And at times we see their activism
is motivated by fear about the future,

00:18:39.698 --> 00:18:42.201
anger towards the older generations

00:18:42.201 --> 00:18:46.246
who did so little to prevent
this moment from happening.

00:18:46.288 --> 00:18:50.959
What message do you give
to young activists like Vanessa,

00:18:51.001 --> 00:18:52.628
and others around the world,

00:18:52.628 --> 00:18:55.714
to keep them in the streets
demanding change?"

00:18:57.049 --> 00:18:59.051
JF: Join them in the streets.

00:18:59.385 --> 00:19:00.803
That's what I did.

00:19:01.178 --> 00:19:06.058
I went to DC and got arrested five times
with Fire Drill Fridays,

00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:09.937
and we were there for four months
getting arrested every Friday.

00:19:10.604 --> 00:19:13.065
We started out with 13 people
getting arrested

00:19:13.107 --> 00:19:14.274
and by the time we end,

00:19:14.316 --> 00:19:16.235
there was a little less than a thousand.

00:19:16.276 --> 00:19:18.237
I mean, it really grew.

00:19:20.197 --> 00:19:23.075
Words don't matter, show with your body,

00:19:23.117 --> 00:19:24.910
put your body on the line,

00:19:24.952 --> 00:19:28.956
and show them that you are willing
to join them in the streets.

00:19:28.956 --> 00:19:30.624
We need huge numbers.

00:19:30.666 --> 00:19:34.128
Come and join us on December 2
in Washington, DC.

00:19:35.838 --> 00:19:37.214
PM: I plan to be there.

00:19:37.631 --> 00:19:42.386
Jane, we keep hearing a lot
about the seven-year timeline,

00:19:42.428 --> 00:19:44.930
and we know that’s the timeline we have

00:19:44.930 --> 00:19:48.684
to reduce carbon emissions
to where they need to be by 2030

00:19:48.726 --> 00:19:52.229
to get to net zero by 2050.

00:19:52.688 --> 00:19:55.190
In order to do whatever is necessary,

00:19:55.190 --> 00:19:57.985
we have to imagine there's a better time.

00:19:58.026 --> 00:19:59.862
Some people have actually ...

00:19:59.903 --> 00:20:03.157
a wonderful Indigenous leader
actually proposed the question,

00:20:03.157 --> 00:20:06.410
Jade Begay, she said,
"What if our best times are ahead?"

00:20:07.035 --> 00:20:09.079
And I frankly had never thought of that.

00:20:09.121 --> 00:20:14.168
We think so much of the worse future
we are rushing and hurtling towards.

00:20:14.168 --> 00:20:18.797
How do you envision what might be
our future, seven years from now?

00:20:19.506 --> 00:20:21.258
JF: Well, it's going to take a while

00:20:21.300 --> 00:20:23.761
because of all the heat
that's already been baked in

00:20:23.761 --> 00:20:28.098
because fossil fuel executives lied to us
about what their emissions were doing,

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:31.018
you know, which they knew
for four decades.

00:20:31.059 --> 00:20:34.813
But when I think of how the world
will be generations from now,

00:20:34.855 --> 00:20:38.442
if we do what science says we have to do,

00:20:38.442 --> 00:20:39.985
I see healthier people

00:20:39.985 --> 00:20:42.321
because the air we breathe
and the water we drink

00:20:42.321 --> 00:20:45.073
and the food we eat
will be clean and safe.

00:20:45.073 --> 00:20:46.950
Nature will begin to return.

00:20:46.992 --> 00:20:50.621
Species on the brink
of extinction will rebound.

00:20:51.079 --> 00:20:53.749
Coral reefs and dense forests

00:20:53.791 --> 00:20:57.669
and snow-capped mountains
that we can see from a distance

00:20:57.669 --> 00:21:00.839
will bring joy to us once again.

00:21:00.881 --> 00:21:05.010
And our little species, Homo sapiens,

00:21:05.052 --> 00:21:08.722
will feel happier and less anxious.

00:21:08.722 --> 00:21:13.852
Because, and I hope that along
with confronting the climate crisis,

00:21:13.894 --> 00:21:17.523
we will have confronted
economic inequality

00:21:17.564 --> 00:21:19.775
and misogyny and racism.

00:21:19.817 --> 00:21:22.611
Because, you know,
the climate crisis has happened

00:21:22.653 --> 00:21:28.242
because of a mindset of arrogance,
greed and hierarchy.

00:21:28.283 --> 00:21:31.495
You know, some things like,
white men are more important,

00:21:31.495 --> 00:21:33.288
they're on the top of the hierarchy,

00:21:33.288 --> 00:21:37.459
they matter more than people of color
or women or the natural world.

00:21:38.252 --> 00:21:41.672
But there would be no climate
crisis without racism.

00:21:42.214 --> 00:21:45.717
There would be no climate crisis
without patriarchy.

00:21:45.759 --> 00:21:49.638
So it's going to be a beautiful
future if we make it so.

00:21:49.680 --> 00:21:52.307
And we are the last generation
that can do so.

00:21:53.100 --> 00:21:55.602
And that is an awesome responsibility.

00:21:56.019 --> 00:21:58.856
And this is an all-hands-on-deck moment.

00:21:58.856 --> 00:22:00.566
So let's all join together.

00:22:01.817 --> 00:22:06.363
PM: It's an awesome responsibility
and an awesome opportunity

00:22:06.363 --> 00:22:07.865
for us to do this together.

00:22:07.865 --> 00:22:09.658
JF: Opportunity, yes, good.

00:22:10.158 --> 00:22:13.787
PM: I just have to ask you, Jane,
before we say goodbye,

00:22:13.787 --> 00:22:16.957
because so many people have written
about their concerns

00:22:16.999 --> 00:22:20.627
of knowing that you are battling cancer,

00:22:20.627 --> 00:22:22.921
and we just want to know,
you look fantastic,

00:22:22.963 --> 00:22:24.423
but how is your health?

00:22:26.925 --> 00:22:30.637
JF: Well for the week
when I have chemo, you know, it's ...

00:22:32.139 --> 00:22:33.724
I don't feel like my old self,

00:22:33.724 --> 00:22:35.809
but after that, I feel good.

00:22:35.851 --> 00:22:38.437
In fact, I feel better than ever
because, you know,

00:22:38.437 --> 00:22:41.315
that cancer was in me,
and it was kind of eating away at me,

00:22:41.356 --> 00:22:44.151
and I was tired all the time,
and I’m not anymore.

00:22:44.192 --> 00:22:46.987
I got strong just in time
for the midterm elections.

00:22:48.488 --> 00:22:49.990
I appreciate people's thoughts.

00:22:50.032 --> 00:22:54.036
I was hoping my hair would fall out,
or I was going to shave my head

00:22:54.077 --> 00:22:56.121
and tattoo "climate emergency"

00:22:56.163 --> 00:22:59.291
on this new real estate
that I was going to reclaim.

00:22:59.291 --> 00:23:03.754
But cancer activists don't like
to have issues mixed together,

00:23:03.795 --> 00:23:08.008
so I didn't want to do it if the cancer
community wouldn't stand with me.

00:23:08.050 --> 00:23:09.927
But it's good.

00:23:09.968 --> 00:23:13.221
Thank you for thinking of me,
and I'm just fine, thank you.

00:23:13.972 --> 00:23:18.101
PM: Well, it's just so great
to know that you are well

00:23:18.143 --> 00:23:19.728
and you are continuing this work.

00:23:19.770 --> 00:23:23.565
And as Jane reminds me,
and I'm just going to quote her on this,

00:23:23.607 --> 00:23:27.235
"Activism is the antidote to aging."

00:23:27.235 --> 00:23:30.948
And Jane Fonda is a perfect
example of that working,

00:23:30.989 --> 00:23:33.575
not just for her, but for the world.

00:23:33.575 --> 00:23:36.453
Thank you so much, Jane,
for joining us today.

00:23:37.371 --> 00:23:40.082
JF: Thank you for having me, Pat.
It's great to see you.

